  \"1804-1815:O\",\n    \"1816-1816:B\",\n    \"1817-1823:O\",\n    \"1824-1836:B\",\n    \"1837-1837:O\",\n    \"1838-1854:B\",\n    \"1855-1858:O\",\n    \"1859-1872:B\",\n    \"1873-1881:O\",\n    \"1882-1889:B\",\n    \"1890-1891:O\",\n    \"1892-1894:B\",\n    \"1895-1895:O\",\n    \"1896-1901:B\",\n    \"1902-1905:O\",\n    \"1906-1921:B\",\n    \"1922-1922:U\",\n    \"1923-1926:O\",\n    \"1927-1934:B\",\n    \"1935-1935:U\",\n    \"1936-1938:O\",\n    \"1939-1939:U\",\n    \"1940-1943:B\",\n    \"1944-1944:U\",\n    \"1945-1996:O\",\n    \"1997-2003:U\",\n    \"2004-2019:O\"\n  ],\n  \"teaserSpans\": [],\n  \"candidates\": [\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 124,\n      \"endEvent\": 127,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 106,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 160,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"And it's never going to be true.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"unclear\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The absolute prediction preempts Slick's pending argument, apparently on the strength of previous encounters. However, A has supplied methodological reasons at E113–120 and subsequently explains his restrictive definition of faith at E149–160. It is unclear whether this substitutes for engagement or forcefully restates that definitional disagreement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 150,\n      \"endEvent\": 150,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 144,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 160,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"an epistivist is one who rejects faith as being the most dishonest position it is possible to have.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"unclear\",\n      \"protection\": \"no\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"identity\", \"shame\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The identity affirmation adds a maximal moral condemnation whose comparison with other dishonest positions is not established. Yet E151–159 supplies an explicit definition connecting faith with unsupported, resistant conviction, following the honesty argument at E78–88. Whether the superlative performs independent inferential work remains uncertain; identity alone does not establish protection.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 369,\n      \"endEvent\": 372,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 339,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 417,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"there's nothing you would accept to ever prove you wrong.\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"unclear\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"shame\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A attributes unconditional resistance to his interlocutor without establishing that Slick would reject every possible correction. This could preempt counterexamples to A's account of faith, but it also follows A's explicitly stated definition. Slick's alternative-explanation discussion at E349–351 complicates the attribution; describing another person's alleged protection is not itself conclusive protection.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 708,\n      \"endEvent\": 713,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 695,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 729,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"God is a necessary precondition for the intelligibility and consistency of the universe.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"yes\",\n      \"protection\": \"no\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The affirmation supplies the missing bridge from questions about induction to God's necessity. E706–711 raises a problem for justification but does not establish a divine solution or its uniqueness. The later logic argument at E1526–1573 develops the intended approach without earning that bridge. No merit-independent dismissal is explicit in this episode.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 808,\n      \"endEvent\": 809,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 789,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 874,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"a transcendent being that automatically can't be found in a scientific method?\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"no\",\n      \"protection\": \"yes\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The word 'automatically' expressly excludes scientific detection when answering the demand for evidence. Nondependence on space and time at E804–807 does not establish that effects could never be detectable. This protects against that evidential route, while E872's promised proof leaves other reasoning available; the protected argument is not a slogan.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1256,\n      \"endEvent\": 1257,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1240,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1263,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"in every discussion, the religious side always collapses.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"yes\",\n      \"protection\": \"no\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"ridicule\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The universal victory affirmation helps justify A's confidence without demonstrating the asserted record of every religious discussion. E1243–1252 offers concrete research practices, and E1258–1261 narrows his usual subject toward creationism, but neither establishes universal collapse. No explicit rule makes contrary outcomes confirm the assertion regardless of their merits.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1479,\n      \"endEvent\": 1486,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1405,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1491,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"there's going to be some people who don't accept reason at all\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"no\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A anticipates discounting residual dissent in an expert consensus as unreasonable or scientifically deficient. That could protect the consensus from criticism without examining dissent. However, E1481 also allows other reasons for abstention, and E1455–1458 requires inclusive expertise. The hypothetical does not clearly establish that every substantive minority objection would be dismissed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1520,\n      \"endEvent\": 1521,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1503,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1525,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"you are demonstrating that by repeating it so often.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"unclear\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"shame\", \"identity\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The renewed 'most dishonest position' affirmation makes Slick's repeated attribution appear to demonstrate faith's dishonesty. The missing connection is between repetition and dishonest belief rather than a disputed definition. Earlier evidence-versus-faith explanations supply context, so it remains uncertain whether disagreement itself is being immunizingly recast as confirmation or merely criticized as misrepresentation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1580,\n      \"endEvent\": 1582,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1526,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1594,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"If there is no God, there is no universe.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"yes\",\n      \"protection\": \"yes\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Slick blocks A's proposed godless-universe counterexample by restating the divine necessity being contested. E1567–1573 characterizes logic and requests an atheistic account, but does not establish that no such universe is possible. Calling the response a dodge at E1582 reinforces the exclusion. The surrounding attempted argument does not supply the missing necessity premise.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1610,\n      \"endEvent\": 1615,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1595,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1622,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"Because I don't believe it's possible.\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"yes\",\n      \"protection\": \"yes\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"explanation\": \"After renewed questioning, Slick declines the godless-universe hypothetical because he disbelieves its possibility, then calls justification another topic. A stated personal belief alone would not qualify, but here it excludes a relevant challenge to the proposed proof. The prior logic discussion supplies context without demonstrating impossibility; this is a protected non-slogan response.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1646,\n      \"endEvent\": 1647,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1637,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1656,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"God is a necessary precondition for the universe.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"yes\",\n      \"protection\": \"yes\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Following another formulation of A's counterexample, Slick again rejects it using the conclusion that the universe requires God. The missing reason is why logical or numerical relations cannot obtain without God. E1652 and E1656 promise a demonstration, fairly indicating intended argument, but do not justify excluding the hypothetical before that demonstration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1790,\n      \"endEvent\": 1790,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1781,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1794,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"Then you have faith in your confidence.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"yes\",\n      \"protection\": \"yes\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"ridicule\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A's distinction between confidence and faith is converted immediately into another instance of faith, insulating the attribution from that correction. Earlier induction and continuity arguments explain Slick's concern about assumptions, but this recursive reply supplies no reason why the stated confidence must satisfy either disputed definition. The protection concerns this particular attempted rebuttal.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1906,\n      \"endEvent\": 1919,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1902,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1921,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"he won't be humbled because he doesn't want anything to do with God.\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"unclear\",\n      \"protection\": \"yes\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"shame\", \"fear\", \"identity\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Slick interprets A's continuing rejection as idolatry and unwillingness to be humbled, supplying an internal defect as the explanation for resistance. No independent access to that motivation is supplied. E1917 explicitly qualifies this as opinion and earlier passages criticize specific arguments, but the concluding diagnosis still protects the religious position from this critic's objections.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"contrasts\": [\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1138,\n      \"endEvent\": 1144,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"It's not like there's ever a time that it's closed.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A explains continuing scrutiny through testing, correlation, and independent review, explicitly leaving historical conclusions open to correction. The compact statement summarizes that procedure; it does not independently establish his historical claims.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 916,\n      \"endEvent\": 922,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"a profound trust based on evidence and reason\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Slick supports his proposed biblical meaning of faith with a passage depicting an invitation to inspect wounds. That is relevant textual evidence about usage, independently of whether the narrated event occurred. A later supplies a competing qualification from the following verse at E980–983.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}}
